From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 01:46:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 078/342] arm64: count user time as user time

The kernel recorded the entry from user mode (thread_at_kernel_entry())
only for syscalls, but charged the time since then as kernel time at
every return to user mode. A thread interrupted by the timer or a page
fault got its user time booked as kernel time: `time` on a loop without
syscalls showed 0 s user and 3.7 s system. Record the entry for every
exception taken from EL0, as x86 does in its entry code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_int.cpp | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_int.cpp b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_int.cpp
index 095eae5865..53ccfb20f5 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_int.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_int.cpp
@@ -211,8 +211,13 @@ public:
 		fThread = thread_get_current_thread();
 		if (fThread) {
 			arm64_push_iframe(&fThread->arch_info.iframes, iframe);
-			if ((iframe->spsr & PSR_M_MASK) == PSR_M_EL0t)
+			if ((iframe->spsr & PSR_M_MASK) == PSR_M_EL0t) {
+				// whatever brought the thread into the kernel (an interrupt,
+				// a fault, a syscall), the time until now was user time; the
+				// exit charges the time in here as kernel time
+				thread_at_kernel_entry(system_time());
 				arm64_user_debug_at_kernel_entry();
+			}
 		} else
 			arm64_push_iframe(&gBootFrameStack, iframe);
 	}
@@ -402,7 +407,6 @@ do_sync_handler(iframe * frame)
 				}
 			}
 
-			thread_at_kernel_entry(system_time());
 			thread_get_current_thread()->arch_info.old_x0 = frame->x[0];
 
 			enable_interrupts();
